          </div>

          <p>
            For comparison, a big tub of mainstream whey often comes in somewhere around $1.20 to $1.70 per 25 g. One Devotion shake a day therefore costs roughly $15 to $30 a month more than a budget whey. That is the price of the flavor work and the baking performance, and whether it is worth it depends entirely on how much you value those.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math looks better if you think about what Devotion replaces. If it gets you to swap a bakery muffin for a protein muffin, or keeps you away from a sugary dessert, the premium may pay for itself in food you no longer buy. If it simply replaces a cheaper protein shake you were already drinking, it is hard to justify.
          </p>

          <p>
            To keep costs down, subscribe to the flavor you use most, buy sample packs only for testing, and skip the 12-packs except for travel. If cost per gram is your main concern, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide lists far cheaper options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and Label Transparency
          </h2>

          <p>
            Devotion's product pages describe the protein as third-party tested and state there is no amino spiking, alongside a GMP and testing graphic that shows a Eurofins logo. That is a reasonable start, and the absence of free amino acids on the ingredient list supports the no-spiking claim. Devotion does not, however, advertise NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport status.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters for one group: drug-tested athletes. A brand's own testing, even through a reputable lab, is not the same as a certification program with a public database you can search by product and flavor. If you compete under anti-doping rules, choose a product certified by one of those programs instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the label itself is clear. Devotion discloses the protein sources, the approximate whey-to-casein ratio and every ingredient, and lists allergens plainly. The only undisclosed amounts are in the enzyme blend, which is standard practice. The label is long, with gums, maltodextrin and two sweeteners, so it does not fit a minimalist brief.
          </p>

          <p>
            One small caution: we found minor copy errors and inconsistencies between some Devotion and Amazon listings, such as an ingredient field on one Amazon page listing only whey protein. Rely on the supplement facts panel on the pouch you receive rather than the product page, especially if you have allergies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Devotion vs. PEScience and Other Flavor Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            The closest comparison is <strong>PEScience Select Protein</strong>, which also blends casein and whey and is sold on its flavor. Select lists about 24 g of protein per scoop for flavors like Gourmet Vanilla, versus about 20 g for Devotion, and in the large 55-serving tub it often works out cheaper per 25 g. If you mostly drink shakes, PEScience gives you more protein for the money. Our <a href="/pescience-protein-powder-review/">PEScience protein powder review</a> has the full breakdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Devotion has the edge for baking and for unusual flavors. PEScience's blend is built on milk protein isolate and also behaves well in recipes, but Devotion's savory Buttery Blend and its recipe-first marketing make it the more natural pick for people who cook with protein several times a week. PEScience offers a vegan line, which Devotion does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against straight whey brands with dessert flavors, Devotion trades protein density for texture. A pure whey isolate typically delivers 25 g of protein in a similar calorie count, so it is the better pure shake. Devotion's 20 g scoop with casein is the better ingredient for cooking. Choose based on whether you will drink it or bake with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other flavor-led brands, such as Clean Simple Eats, aim at a similar audience. Our <a href="/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/">Clean Simple Eats review</a> is worth reading if you are comparing brands with a recipe-heavy, lifestyle focus.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip Devotion
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Devotion if your main goal is hitting a high protein target cheaply. At about 20 g per scoop and roughly $2.30 to $2.70 per 25 g, it is one of the more expensive ways to buy protein, and heavy users who drink two or three shakes a day will feel that quickly. A large tub of mainstream whey costs far less per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are a drug-tested athlete. Devotion says its products are third-party tested, but it does not advertise a sport certification with a searchable database. Choose an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport product instead, and confirm the exact flavor before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you avoid artificial sweeteners, carrageenan or soy. The range uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, the fiber blend includes carrageenan, and the label lists soy as an allergen. Devotion's separate FlexFlavors packets are stevia-sweetened, but the protein itself is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip it, or check with a clinician first, if you have a milk allergy, kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication that affects protein needs. Devotion contains milk proteins, and any change to your protein intake in those situations should go through a doctor or registered dietitian. Nothing on this page is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per pouch, not per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $65 pouch with 30 servings looks similar to other tubs until you factor in the 20 g scoop. Work out cost per 25 g of protein: Devotion lands around $2.30 to $2.70, well above mainstream whey. Comparing that way shows whether you are paying for protein or for flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping all the flour for protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even a baking-friendly blend turns dense and dry if it replaces all the flour in a recipe. Replace about a third to a half, add moisture such as yogurt, banana or pumpkin, and bake a little lower and longer. Devotion's own recipes are a sensible starting point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a full pouch of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flavor is the whole reason to buy Devotion, and taste is personal. Devotion sells sample packs and variety packs of single servings for exactly this. Spend a few dollars testing two or three flavors before committing to a 2 lb pouch you might not finish. Single-serve packets also travel well.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming it is a short-label powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Devotion's ingredient list includes gums, carrageenan, maltodextrin, sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and the label lists milk and soy. That is fine for most people, but it is not a minimalist product. If you want a two-ingredient protein, choose an unflavored whey isolate and add your own flavor instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating brand testing as sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Devotion says its products are third-party tested, which is good, but that is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Drug-tested athletes need a product listed in one of those public databases, checked by exact product and flavor before every purchase, because listings can change.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Devotion protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a well-designed flavored protein that is especially good for baking, thanks to its whey isolate and micellar casein blend. Each scoop lists about 20 g of protein in roughly 100 to 110 calories with no added sugar. Its weakness is cost: at roughly $2.30 to $2.70 per 25 g of protein, it is expensive for a modest scoop.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Devotion protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 20 g per scoop, from a blend of roughly 80 percent whey protein isolate and 20 percent micellar casein, per Devotion's labels at the time of writing. A 2 lb pouch holds about 30 servings. Calories run about 100 to 110 per scoop depending on flavor, with 1 g of MCT and no added sugar.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Devotion use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Devotion's protein powders are sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per the ingredient lists at the time of writing. Devotion's separate FlexFlavors product, a range of zero-calorie flavor packets, is sweetened with stevia instead. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, the protein itself will not suit you, so check the label before buying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best Devotion flavor?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Angel Food Cake is the flagship and the most versatile, working in shakes, oats and most sweet baking. Buttery Blend is the pick for savory recipes, and Brownie Batter is the chocolate option. Taste is personal and we did not taste-test the range, so sample packs are the cheapest way to find your favorite.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with Devotion protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, and baking is its main strength. The micellar casein and gum system help baked goods hold moisture, so pancakes and muffins stay softer than with pure whey. Replace roughly a third to a half of the flour with protein, add a moist ingredient, and bake a little lower and longer than the original recipe.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Devotion protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Devotion says its protein is third-party tested with no amino spiking, and its product pages show a Eurofins testing graphic. It does not advertise NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport status at the time of writing. Drug-tested athletes should choose a product listed in one of those certification databases instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Devotion sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Devotion's 2 lb pouches and single-serve packs are listed on Amazon, sold by Devotion Nutrition and fulfilled by Amazon at the time of writing, with prices matching Devotion's own site, including a subscription discount. Some flavors go out of stock on the brand's site, so Amazon is a useful backup for the flagship flavors.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

